// runtime.metajs -- the SHARED layer-2 runtime, imported by every *-rt.metajs.
//
// Layer 2 is the per-language runtime written in MetaJS and compiled by
// languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf with -rt-lib into <lang>-rt.ll, which the
// language's own -exe links next to lib/runtime.ll (the C floor). Eleven such
// files exist, and docs/runtime-merge-plan.md measured that 29 extern names have
// between two and nine implementations of the SAME function, costing 1,427 lines.
// This file is where those merge.
//
// The mechanism is the one ruby proved with `import "./regex.js"`: a layer-2 file
// may import a shared MetaJS source, metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf resolves the relative
// path, and -rt-lib's lazy boot runs both into the SAME pinned scope. So a
// definition here is indistinguishable from one written in the importing file.
//
// ----- how a language parameterises a shared body ----------------------------
//
// An extern's signature is fixed by the emitter (js_jadd(l, r)), so a shared body
// cannot take a knob object the way lib/interp-core.js's `core` does. Instead it
// calls HOOK functions that the importing file defines, all named rtk*:
//
//     rtkStr(v)   the language's rt.javaString - how `+` renders an operand once
//                 the other side is a string. Every language differs here and
//                 always will; this is the one irreducible knob of js_jadd.
//     rtkNum(v)   the language's rt.toNumber - how an operand becomes a double.
//                 Also irreducible: PHP's raises on an array, Dart's answers NaN
//                 for a non-number, Kotlin's answers 0 for null and NaN for
//                 undefined because kotlin.Unit arrives as undefined.
//
// Top-level function declarations hoist and both files land in one scope, so the
// hook may be defined after the import and in the other file. Every name here is
// prefixed rt / rtk so nothing collides with a language's own helpers.
//
// ----- MetaJS rules that shape the code below --------------------------------
//
// docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md: a local keeps the TYPE CLASS it is first assigned;
// there is no ASI, so every `return` carries its value on the same line and a
// value-less early return is BRACED; no for..in, no splice, no split/join,
// array.length is not assignable, and there are no exponent literals.

// ===================================================== int32 ================
//
// Go: isInt32Value (abnf/jsrt.go:8420). NINE layer-2 files carried a private copy
// of this predicate under nine names (csIsInt32Value, dtIsInt32, jvIsInt32Value,
// goIsInt32Value, phIsInt32, pyAIsInt32, swIsInt32, k1IsInt32Value, rbIsInt32).
// They differed in three ways, all of them measured to be the same function:
//   - Math.trunc(n) !== n  vs  Math.floor(n) !== n. They disagree on which value
//     a non-integer maps to (-1 vs -2 for -1.5) and agree on the only thing asked,
//     which is whether it equals n.
//   - `!=` (python) vs `!==` (everyone else). Both operands are numbers, so the
//     loose compare never coerces.
//   - the low bound written `n >= 0 - 2147483648` (no negative literals in the
//     dialect at the time) vs python's `n >= -2147483648`.
function rtIsInt32(n) {
    if (n !== n) { return false }
    if (Math.trunc(n) !== n) { return false }
    if (n > 2147483647) { return false }
    return n >= 0 - 2147483648
}

// Go: float64(int32(x)), i.e. ToInt32. Eight private copies (csWrap32, jvWrap32,
// goWrap32, dtWrap32, swWrap32, phWrap32, ktNorm(x,32,0), and python/ruby's bare
// `| 0`). Two shapes existed:
//   - csharp/java guarded NaN and the infinities to 0 FIRST; the other six did
//     not. `x - Math.floor(x/2^32)*2^32` on a NaN or an infinity is NaN, so the
//     guard is a real difference - it is just unreachable from js_jadd, whose
//     float arm has already taken every non-finite operand. Kept, because it is
//     the correct answer and costs nothing.
//   - the fold-down test written `m >= 2147483648` (csharp/java) vs
//     `m > 2147483647` (the rest). Identical on an integer m, and m is always an
//     integer here because x is.
function rtWrap32(x) {
    if (x !== x) { return 0 }
    if (x == Infinity) { return 0 }
    if (x == 0 - Infinity) { return 0 }
    var m = x - Math.floor(x / 4294967296) * 4294967296
    if (m >= 2147483648) { m = m - 4294967296 }
    return m
}

// ===================================================== js_jadd ==============
//
// EXPORTED DIRECTLY. -rt-lib reads its exported surface off the SOURCE TEXT -
// every line starting in column 0 with `function js_` - because a MetaJS function
// is a value in a scope and its name is not in the IR. It used to scan only the
// MAIN file, so a `function js_jadd` written here compiled, ran correctly under
// llvm.Run, and was UNDEFINED AT LINK TIME; the matrix and --cross are blind to
// that and tests/clang-check.sh was the only gate that saw it. The scan now
// covers the imported files too (metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf, the -rt-lib block), so
// a shared extern is written here under its real name and needs no forwarder.
//
// Go: js_jadd (abnf/jsrt.go:5151). Java/C# `+`: string concat when either side is
// a string, a 32 bit wrapping add when both sides are int32-valued, a boxed
// double when either side is one, a plain double add otherwise.
//
// NINE implementations merged. The differences found, and how each was classified
// (docs/runtime-merge-plan.md asks for this list; it is the deliverable):
//
//  1. KNOB - the string renderer. csJavaStr / dtJavaStr / jpStr / goJavaStr /
//     phJavaStr / pyAJavaStr / swJavaStr / k1JStr / rbJStr. Nine genuinely
//     different functions: java renders an array as `[I@1b6d3586` and recurses
//     into objects, kotlin renders a Char as its character, ruby spells nil
//     "null", go delegates to gopStr. Irreducible - this is rtkStr.
//
//  2. KNOB - the numeric coercion. csNum / dtNumOf / jvNum / goNum /
//     phNumOf(phToNum(v)) / pyNum / swNum / ktNum / rbToF. Also genuinely
//     different, and the differences are load-bearing: dart answers NaN for a
//     non-number where csharp answers 0; kotlin answers 0 for null and NaN for
//     undefined (a comment at ktNum records that answering 0 for undefined made
//     `size + f()` of a Unit-returning f print the size where the Go twin prints
//     NaN); php's raises "unsupported operand type: array used as a number".
//     Irreducible - this is rtkNum.
//
//  3. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the float arm's two shapes. The plan's headline example
//     is java's `jvmArith("+", l, r)` against swift's
//     `flo(swNum(l) + swNum(r), floStyle(floIs(l) ? l : r))`, and asks whether
//     they agree on every input. They do, and the reason is worth recording:
//     jvmArith/csFloArith are `floOp(0, operand(l), operand(r))`, floOp is
//     abnf/jsrtjvm.go:285 which calls rt.jvmArith, and rt.jvmArith (jsrtjvm.go:176)
//     is `jsJFlo{f: rt.toNumber(l)+rt.toNumber(r), sty: jvmStyleOf(l, r)}` whenever
//     either side is a box - the same expression, spelled through the floor. The
//     one place the two spellings could part is the operand mapping: java's
//     jvmOperand is `floIs(v) ? v : jvNum(v)` and is exactly rtkNum, while
//     csharp's csFloOperand passes a non-flo operand through UNCHANGED for the
//     floor's own rt.toNumber to coerce. Those agree on every type csNum and
//     rt.toNumber both handle (flo, char, sized int, number, bool) and part only
//     on an object/array/undefined operand, where rt.toNumber says NaN and csNum
//     says 0 - see the DEFECT below.
//
//  4. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the style argument. go wrote goStyleOf(l, r), kotlin
//     wrote k1StyleOf(l, r), swift and dart wrote floStyle(floIs(l) ? l : r).
//     goStyleOf and k1StyleOf are the same function EXCEPT for their final arm,
//     which returns 1 (floGo) in go and 0 (floJava) in kotlin - and that arm is
//     reachable only when NEITHER side is a box, which is precisely the case the
//     caller has already excluded. Dead code, correct-for-its-language in both,
//     never observed. Merged to the swift/dart spelling.
//
//  5. KNOB, documented - php, python and ruby have NO float arm at all. Each of
//     the three carries a comment saying why: php's emitter emits js_jadd only
//     for its own loop counters, python's reaches it only through
//     makeStrConcat/makeFString/emitSpecText which seed the chain with
//     emitStr(b, "") so the string arm always wins, and ruby's emits it only as
//     js_jadd(i, 1) on an internal counter. The arm is therefore unreachable
//     rather than wrong, and the merged body simply HAS it: a superset that costs
//     those three nothing, and that the day one of them starts emitting js_jadd on
//     a user value will already be right.
//
//  6. DEFECT (csharp), fixed here - see point 3. csharp reached the float arm
//     through the floor's rt.toNumber and now reaches it through csNum, whose
//     last arm answers 0 where rt.toNumber answers NaN, for an object, an array,
//     or undefined. The Go twin is the oracle and says NaN. Measured: the only
//     C# operand that can be a bare object on the double path is a `dynamic` or a
//     boxed `object`, which csharp-to-llvm-ir.abnf does not emit, so the arm is
//     unreachable today and neither half changed an answer. Recorded rather than
//     tuned: csNum's fallback should be NaN, not 0, and that is csharp-rt.metajs's
//     own bug rather than js_jadd's.
function js_jadd(l, r) {
    if (typeof l == "string" || typeof r == "string") { return rtkStr(l) + rtkStr(r) }
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) { return flo(rtkNum(l) + rtkNum(r), floStyle(floIs(l) ? l : r)) }
    var ln = rtkNum(l)
    var rn = rtkNum(r)
    if (rtIsInt32(ln) && rtIsInt32(rn)) { return rtWrap32(ln + rn) }
    return ln + rn
}

// ===================================================== js_is_type ===========
//
// Exported directly, for the reason recorded at js_jadd.
//
// Go: js_is_type -> rt.isTypeName (abnf/jsrt.go:8432). `x is T` / `x as? T`:
// strip a generic argument list and a trailing `?`, answer the nullable flag for
// a null, then a fixed table of well-known type names, then walk __class/__super.
//
// SIX of the eight implementations merged here - csharp, java, kotlin, python,
// ruby, swift. go and dart are deliberately NOT merged; see the end of this note.
// The differences found, and how each was classified:
//
//  1. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the `<` strip and the `?` strip. csharp/java/python/
//     ruby/swift inlined indexOf/substring; kotlin called k4bStripAngle /
//     k4bEndsQ / k4bStripQ, which are the same three lines under names. csharp
//     and java guarded the `?` test with a nested `if (nm.length > 0) {...}`
//     where python/ruby/swift used `&&`; identical.
//
//  2. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the null test. csharp/java/kotlin/swift wrote
//     `v === undefined || v === null`; python called pyIsNone and ruby rbIsNil,
//     and BOTH of those are exactly `v === null || v === undefined`. Python's
//     None and Ruby's nil are the JS null in this value model, so no knob.
//
//  3. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the object walk. csharp/java tested
//     `typeof v != "object"` then `Array.isArray(v)`; python/ruby called
//     pyIsObj/rbIsObj, which are those two tests; kotlin/swift called
//     ktIsObj/swIsObj, which admit an array - but an array's `__class` is
//     undefined, so the walk exits on the first step and the answer is the same
//     false.
//
//  4. KNOB - the Char test. csharp, java and kotlin have a boxed Char
//     (csIsChar/jvIsChar/ktIsChar; csharp's and java's are byte-for-byte the same
//     function). python, ruby and swift have no Char in their value model at all
//     and omitted the arm. rtkIsChar, answering false for those three, restores
//     the oracle's arm order for the three that have one and costs the others a
//     predicate call.
//
//  5. KNOB - the array test. csharp/java/kotlin/python/ruby all use
//     Array.isArray; swift's arrays are OBJECTS carrying a length, so swIsArr is
//     a real difference. rtkIsArr.
//
//  6. NOT A DIFFERENCE, though it looked like one - the numeric predicate.
//     csharp/java/kotlin used isPlainNum (`typeof v == "number"` AND NOT a sized
//     integer AND NOT a boxed double), swift inlined the same three tests, and
//     python and ruby used a BARE `typeof v == "number"`. The oracle is
//     `v.(float64)`, which a jsGInt and a jsJFlo both fail, so the bare test
//     would be a defect - except that python-rt.metajs and ruby-rt.metajs
//     mention sintIs/floIs ZERO times each: neither language's value model has a
//     tagged number, so on their values the two spellings cannot differ. The
//     strict form is shared, and it is the correct one the day either language
//     grows a box.
//
//  7. DIVERGENCE FROM THE ORACLE, kept deliberately - the walk bound. csharp,
//     java, kotlin and swift cap the __class/__super walk at 64 steps; python and
//     ruby loop until the chain ends, which is what jsrt.go:8472 does. The
//     oracle HANGS on a cyclic __super and the guard answers false, and neither
//     C#, Java, Kotlin, Swift, Python nor Ruby can express a 65-deep hierarchy in
//     a program this tree compiles. The guard is taken for all six: it is the
//     safe answer, and the case that distinguishes them is unconstructible.
//
//  8. NOT MERGED - go. go-rt.metajs's js_is_type is a different function, not a
//     variant: it does not strip `<` or `?`, answers FALSE (not opt) for a null,
//     has no numeric/Char/nullable arms at all, and reads the class name through
//     goStrOf. Go's type switch has none of the nullable-generic surface the
//     other six share, so there is nothing to parameterise - merging it would
//     mean adding six knobs to skip six arms.
//
//  9. NOT MERGED - dart. dart-rt.metajs's js_is_type is
//     `return dtTypeName(v, t)`, a Dart-specific type-name resolver. Same reason.
function js_is_type(v, t) {
    var nm = t
    var lt = nm.indexOf("<")
    if (lt >= 0) { nm = nm.substring(0, lt) }
    var opt = false
    if (nm.length > 0 && nm.charAt(nm.length - 1) == "?") {
        nm = nm.substring(0, nm.length - 1)
        opt = true
    }
    if (v === undefined) { return opt }
    if (v === null) { return opt }
    // Kotlin's Char is its own type: `c is Char` holds, `c is Int` does not.
    // Tested before the name switch, exactly as jsrt.go:8448 does.
    if (rtkIsChar(v)) {
        if (nm == "Char") { return true }
        if (nm == "Character") { return true }
        if (nm == "Any") { return true }
        return nm == "Comparable"
    }
    if (nm == "Any" || nm == "Object") { return true }
    var plain = rtIsPlainNum(v)
    if (nm == "Int" || nm == "Integer" || nm == "Long" || nm == "Short" || nm == "Byte" || nm == "Char" || nm == "Character") {
        if (!plain) { return false }
        return Math.trunc(v) == v
    }
    if (nm == "Double" || nm == "Float" || nm == "Number") { return plain }
    if (nm == "String" || nm == "CharSequence") { return typeof v == "string" }
    if (nm == "Boolean") { return typeof v == "boolean" }
    if (nm == "List" || nm == "MutableList" || nm == "Collection" || nm == "Array") { return rtkIsArr(v) }
    if (typeof v != "object") { return false }
    var cls = v["__class"]
    var guard = 0
    while (guard < 64) {
        guard = guard + 1
        if (cls === undefined) { return false }
        if (cls === null) { return false }
        if (typeof cls != "object") { return false }
        if (cls["__name"] === nm) { return true }
        cls = cls["__super"]
    }
    return false
}

// The oracle's `v.(float64)`: an ordinary double, which a SIZED INTEGER (tag 13)
// and a BOXED DOUBLE (tag 14) are not, even though `typeof` calls both "number".
// Was csIsPlainNum / jvIsPlainNum / k4bIsPlainNum (three identical bodies) and
// swift's inline spelling of the same three tests.
function rtIsPlainNum(v) {
    if (typeof v != "number") { return false }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return false }
    return !floIs(v)
}

// ===================================================== js_dict_new ==========
//
// Go: js_dict_new (abnf/jsrt.go:6369). The empty dict / map / Map / Dictionary
// every language builds: a marked object with two PARALLEL, INSERTION-ORDERED
// arrays. The order is the observable part - Python 3.7+, Ruby, Dart's Map, C#'s
// Dictionary and Swift's ordered rendering all depend on it - which is why this
// is not a JS object with real properties.
//
// SIX implementations merged, and the difference list for this one is EMPTY.
// csharp, dart, go and swift each wrote the object literal inline; python and
// ruby delegated to a pyNewDict / rbNewDict that returns the same literal. All
// six are byte-for-byte identical, and identical to the oracle. That is worth
// stating rather than skipping: the 29-name table counts implementations, not
// disagreements, and a row can be six copies of one line. This one was.
function js_dict_new() { return {__dict: true, keys: [], vals: []} }

// ===================================================== the object test ======
//
// "not null, not undefined, and typeof object". dtIsObj / ktIsObj / swIsObj were
// three byte-identical bodies; csharp, java and php inlined the same three tests
// at every site. rbIsObj and pyIsObj add `&& !Array.isArray(v)`, and that fourth
// test is NOT merged in: every caller here walks __class / __super, an array
// carries neither, so the walk exits on its first step and the answer is the same
// either way. The name is deliberately rt (shared) and not rtk (knob): there is no
// language for which this is a different question.
function rtIsObj(v) {
    if (v === undefined) { return false }
    if (v === null) { return false }
    return typeof v == "object"
}

// The receiver-prepend every dispatcher does before `apply`. Was csPrepend,
// jpPrepend, pyDPrepend (three byte-identical bodies), jvPrepend, k4bPrepend, and
// an inline `var av = [recv] ...` loop in php-rt and swift-rt. k4bPrepend was the
// only one that guarded a null/undefined `args`; the guard is kept, because it is
// the answer a caller with no argument array wants and costs the others two
// compares on a path that already allocates.
function rtPrepend(v, args) {
    var out = []
    out.push(v)
    if (args === undefined) { return out }
    if (args === null) { return out }
    var i = 0
    while (i < args.length) {
        out.push(args[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// ===================================================== the Char box =========
//
// Go: jsChar (abnf/jsrt.go:6077). C#'s char, Java's char and Kotlin's Char are a
// type of their own - `c is Char` holds where `c is Int` does not, and `'a' + 1`
// is an Int while `"" + 'a'` is "a" - so the three languages box one, and they
// boxed it three times: csChar / jvChar / ktChar were byte-for-byte identical, and
// so were csIsChar / jvIsChar.
//
// ktIsChar was the one that read differently - `ktIsObj(v) && typeof v["__char"]
// == "number"` against csharp's and java's `v["__char"] !== undefined` - and it is
// the same predicate: nothing but rtCharBox ever writes __char, and it writes a
// number. Merged to the strict spelling, which is also the cheaper one to be wrong
// about.
//
// Dart, Go, PHP, Python, Ruby and Swift have no Char in their value model at all;
// their rtkIsChar answers a constant false and they never call rtCharBox.
function rtCharBox(code) {
    var o = {}
    o["__char"] = code
    return o
}

function rtIsCharBox(v) {
    if (!rtIsObj(v)) { return false }
    return typeof v["__char"] == "number"
}

// ===================================================== js_char ==============
//
// Go: `js_char` (abnf/jsrt.go:6077) is `jsChar{code: int32(a[0])}`. THREE
// implementations, and all three were the same one-line delegation to the boxer
// above: `return csChar(code_raw)` / `jvChar` / `ktChar`.
//
// The parameter keeps its `_raw` suffix, which is not cosmetic: -rt-lib reads the
// signature off this source line and a name ending in _raw marks the argument as
// an UNBOXED integer, so renaming it would hand the boxer a value handle.
//
// NOT MERGED - swift. swift-rt's js_char is a different function: Swift has no
// Char box (a Character is a String), so it converts a code POINT to UTF-16,
// emitting a surrogate pair above 0xFFFF. It keeps its own definition, which wins
// over this one because a layer-2 file's top level runs after its imports.
//
// A DIVERGENCE FROM THE ORACLE, recorded not fixed: the oracle truncates the code
// to int32 and none of the three does. Unreachable - every emitter feeds js_char a
// character literal's code or a `Char(i)` conversion the front end has already
// range-checked - and narrowing here would be a guess about which language's
// overflow rule to apply.
function js_char(code_raw) { return rtCharBox(code_raw) }

// ===================================================== js_char_code =========
//
// Go: `js_char_code` (abnf/jsrt.go:6080) is `rt.wrapNum(rt.toNumber(v))` - plain
// toNumber, nothing more. THREE implementations:
//
//  1. NOT A DIFFERENCE - csharp's `csIsChar(v) ? v["__char"] : csNum(v)`. csNum's
//     SECOND arm is already `csIsChar(v) -> v["__char"]`, and the arm before it
//     (floIs) cannot hold for an object, so the leading test is dead: the whole
//     body is csNum(v).
//  2. NOT A DIFFERENCE - kotlin's bare `ktNum(v)`, which is the oracle spelled
//     directly. ktNum's first arm unboxes a Char.
//  3. NOT MERGED - swift. `typeof v != "string" ? sintNum(v) : v.charCodeAt(0)`,
//     with NaN for an empty string. Swift has no Char box and swNum on a string is
//     parseFloat, so rtkNum is the wrong function here; swift keeps its own.
//
// So the merged body is rtkNum, which IS each language's rt.toNumber.
function js_char_code(v) { return rtkNum(v) }

// ===================================================== js_supercall =========
//
// Go: js_supercall (abnf/jsrt.go:5058). `super.m(...)`: walk __super from the
// class the caller already resolved, and call the first callable found with the
// receiver PREPENDED to the argument list.
//
// FOUR of the five implementations merged - csharp, java, kotlin, ruby. The
// differences found:
//
//  1. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the callable test. csIsFn, ktIsFn and rbIsFn are three
//     byte-identical `typeof v == "function"`, and java inlined
//     `m !== undefined && typeof m == "function"`, whose first half is implied by
//     the second (typeof undefined is "undefined"). No knob.
//
//  2. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the walk-exit test. csharp/java wrote the three tests
//     inline, kotlin called ktIsObj and ruby rbIsObj; see rtIsObj above.
//
//  3. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the prepend. csPrepend / jpPrepend / k4bPrepend and an
//     inline loop in ruby; see rtPrepend above.
//
//  4. KNOB - the call itself. csharp, java and ruby all spell it
//     `m.apply(undefined, argv)`: the oracle's `rt.call(m, jsUndef, ...)`, `this`
//     deliberately undefined because the receiver travels as argv[0]. Kotlin
//     cannot: k4bCallAcc sets the k4bThis global around the call, which is how a
//     Kotlin accessor declared in a superclass sees its receiver. rtkCall.
//
//  5. DIVERGENCE FROM THE ORACLE, kept - the walk bound. csharp/java/kotlin cap it
//     at 64 and ruby does not; the oracle walks until nil. Same decision as
//     js_is_type, for the same reason and with the same cost: a cyclic __super
//     hangs the oracle and answers "unknown super method" here, and no 65-deep
//     hierarchy is expressible in a program this tree compiles.
//
//  6. NOT MERGED - js. js-rt's js_supercall is the one that must NOT take the 64
//     cap: a JavaScript `class A extends B` chain of 65 IS constructible, and a
//     program that builds one is not exotic. It also normalises the method name
//     through jvStr first, because JS is the only language here whose emitter can
//     hand js_supercall a computed name. It keeps its own definition.
function js_supercall(sup, self, name, args) {
    var m = rtFindMethod(sup, name)
    if (typeof m == "function") { return rtkCall(m, self, rtPrepend(self, args)) }
    fail("unknown super method '" + name + "'")
    return undefined
}

// ===================================================== the method walk ======
//
// The __class / __super walk that resolves a method name to a callable, or to
// undefined. It is what js_supercall above IS, and it is also the tail of
// js_mcall in csharp, java, php and swift - SIX copies of the same eleven lines,
// which is the honest merge that js_mcall's row in the 29-name table conceals.
//
// js_mcall itself does NOT merge, and the difference list says why:
//
//  - csharp, java and swift each open with a String arm over a DIFFERENT method
//    set (csharp has contains/ToUpper/ToLower/Trim, java has none of those and
//    range-checks charAt, swift clamps substring by hand), then a container arm
//    over a different container model (csListMethod / an inline list dispatcher /
//    swListMethod + an inline dict dispatcher).
//  - php has no String or container arm at all and opens on phIsGen.
//  - dart is `return dtMember(recv, name, args)`, one line into a Dart-specific
//    member resolver.
//  - lua is `recv[name].apply(recv, args)` - Lua binds the receiver as `self`
//    rather than prepending it, which is a different calling convention.
//
// Six languages, four calling conventions and five method tables: parameterising
// that would take more knobs than lines. What they DO share is this walk, and
// merging the walk is what removes the duplication that is actually there.
//
// The 64-step cap and the `typeof m == "function"` test are the ones argued at
// js_supercall; csIsFn, ktIsFn and rbIsFn were three byte-identical spellings of
// the test and java, php and swift inlined it.
function rtFindMethod(cls, name) {
    var c = cls
    var guard = 0
    while (guard < 64) {
        guard = guard + 1
        if (!rtIsObj(c)) { return undefined }
        var m = c[name]
        if (typeof m == "function") { return m }
        c = c["__super"]
    }
    return undefined
}

// ===================================================== the dict shape ======
//
// A dict / Map / Dictionary / Hash / Go map is js_dict_new's object: __dict, and
// two parallel insertion-ordered arrays. dtIsDict, goIsDict and swIsDict were
// three spellings of the same four tests; csIsDict wrote `__dict !== undefined`
// where the others wrote `=== true`, and rbIsDict is rbTag(v, "__dict"), which
// is `=== true` without the array checks. All five answer the same question and
// the strict spelling is the shared one; a language whose value model puts a
// __dict on something that is NOT a dict does not exist here.
function rtIsDictObj(v) {
    if (!rtIsObj(v)) { return false }
    if (v["__dict"] !== true) { return false }
    if (!rtkIsArr(v["keys"])) { return false }
    return rtkIsArr(v["vals"])
}

// THE KEY-EQUALITY QUESTION, SETTLED. dtDictFind, rbDictFind and swDictFind were
// three byte-identical copies of this scan, and goDictFind is the same function
// through goKeyEq -> goStrictEq, which is a hand respelling of the floor's own
// strict_eq for numbers (tag 14 -> jf_num_eq, tag 13 -> si_eq) and therefore
// equal to `===` at every pair it can be given.
//
// docs/runtime-merge-plan.md recorded this row as a DISAGREEMENT - "identity in
// dart/python/swift, value equality in csharp and go, so two languages find keys
// three others miss" - and that reading was wrong, for a reason worth writing
// down. `===` in layer 2 is NOT JavaScript identity: it lowers to the floor's
// strict_eq (lib/runtime.c:2112), whose first four lines compare a BOXED DOUBLE
// (tag 14) and a SIZED INTEGER (tag 13) BY VALUE, in the Go twin's own arm order,
// and the floor says so at the site. rt.strictEq (abnf/jsrt.go:1164) is that
// same function, and rt.dictFind (jsrt.go:2348) is what the oracle's js_pyset
// finds keys with. So the bare `===` scan IS the oracle.
//
// Measured rather than argued, because a reading is not a run. Under swiftc
// 6.1.2 (installed), a Swift probe storing at an Int key of 10^12 (a tag 13 box
// in swift-rt's value model), re-storing at a second Int of the same value, at a
// built-up String key, and at a Double key of 1.5:
//
//     real swiftc -O          1 / big2 / 1 20 / 1 2 / 1 8
//     swift-interpreter.abnf  1 / big2 / 1 20 / 1 2 / 1 8
//     swift native -exe       1 / big2 / 1 20 / 1 2 / 1 8
//
// all three identical: the box-valued key is FOUND by the identity-looking scan,
// because the floor compares it by value. The same probe under python3 3.13 and
// both python halves agrees on the numeric and string rows (see the Python bool
// finding below for the one row where all three do not).
//
// The ONE real difference is csharp's, and it is a knob rather than a defect:
// csValueEq's FIRST arm unboxes a {__char} through js_jchareq. A Char box IS an
// ordinary object to strict_eq, so two `'a'` boxes are not `===`, and a C#
// `Dictionary<char, V>` would miss every key without that arm. The oracle has it
// too (rt.strictEq's charCode unboxing, jsrt.go:1167). Dart, Go, Python, Ruby and
// Swift have no Char in their value model, so for them the arm is unreachable and
// the bare scan is the whole function. That is what rtkDictFind selects.
function rtDictFind(keys, k) {
    var i = 0
    while (i < keys.length) {
        if (keys[i] === k) { return i }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

// ===================================================== js_pyset =============
//
// Go: js_pyset (abnf/jsrt.go:6339). `c[k] = v` for a list (a negative index wraps)
// or a dict (find, else append to the parallel arrays). FIVE of the six merged -
// csharp, dart, go, ruby, swift. The full difference list:
//
//  1. KNOB - the dict test. csIsDict / dtIsDict / goIsDict / rbIsDict / swIsDict,
//     now rtkIsDict, and five of the six answer it with the shared rtIsDictObj.
//  2. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the array test, already rtkIsArr.
//  3. KNOB, and NOT the disagreement it was recorded as - the key equality. The
//     whole argument is at rtDictFind above. Four of the five spellings are the
//     same function; csharp's Char arm is the one real knob.
//  4. DIVERGENCE FROM THE ORACLE, now FIXED by merging - the index coercion. The
//     oracle is `int(rt.toNumber(...))` and Go's int() truncates toward zero.
//     dart, go, python and ruby truncated; csharp and swift did NOT, so a
//     fractional index reached the bounds test and the store unrounded. The
//     merged body truncates, which is the oracle.
//     DISCRIMINATING POWER: ZERO, and that is the finding rather than a caveat.
//     ECMA-334 12.8.11.3 makes an array-access index convert to int/uint/long/
//     ulong, and Swift's Array subscript is declared `subscript(index: Int)`
//     (Swift Standard Library, MutableCollection) - so neither language can
//     express a fractional index at all, in any program either grammar accepts.
//     Merging removes the divergence; no test can see it go.
//  5. KNOB, documented - python's __setitem__ dunder arm, which the oracle has
//     (jsrt.go:6340) and the other five deleted as unreachable. python-rt.metajs
//     keeps its own js_pyset, which overrides this one; the dunder arm is the
//     reason, and it is the same reason js_pyget's __getitem__ arm is python's
//     alone. Not a defect in the other five: no other grammar here emits a
//     user-defined subscript operator through js_pyset.
//  6. NOT A DIFFERENCE - ruby's dict arm delegating to rbDictSet, which is this
//     body's find-else-append inlined behind a name.
//
// A PYTHON FINDING SURFACED BY THE PROBE, and SINCE FIXED - it is recorded here
// because this comment used to say "not fixed" and sent someone back to it:
// `d[True] = "t"; d[1] = "one"` is ONE entry in CPython (True == 1 and the hashes
// agree) and used to be TWO in both metacompiler halves, because strict_eq - and
// rt.strictEq with it - make a bool and a number different types. The fix is NOT
// in this shared body and not in strict_eq, which nine languages share: it is a
// KEY ALIAS on python's dict path alone, tried twice (a float box aliases the
// plain number and the number aliases the bool, so {True:"t"}[1.0] is two hops),
// in all three engines - pyKeyAlias in languages/lib/python-rt.metajs, in
// abnf/jsrt.go and in languages/python-interpreter.abnf's core.keyEq. Asserted at
// tests/python-test-full.py bki3-bki16, and all four probe legs - including
// python3 - agree.
function js_pyset(c, k, v) {
    if (rtkIsDict(c)) {
        var keys = c["keys"]
        var i = rtkDictFind(keys, k)
        if (i >= 0) { c["vals"][i] = v } else {
            keys.push(k)
            c["vals"].push(v)
        }
        return undefined
    }
    if (!rtkIsArr(c)) { fail("item assignment on a " + typeof c) }
    var idx = Math.trunc(rtkNum(k))
    if (idx < 0) { idx = idx + c.length }
    if (idx < 0 || idx >= c.length) { fail("list assignment index out of range") }
    c[idx] = v
    return undefined
}

// ============================================ the relational comparison ====
//
// Go: rt.jsCompare (abnf/jsrt.go:1530), the body behind `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=`
// for every operand pair the language's own number tags do not cover. FOUR
// layer-2 copies: jpCompare (java), csCompare (csharp), dtJsCompare (dart) and
// swLoose (swift), plus a fifth in js-rt.metajs (jvCmpPrim) that does not import
// this file. The shape scan grouped three of the four; the fourth and fifth
// differ from them by exactly the thing this section is about.
//
// THE DIFFERENCE LIST:
//
//  1. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the string arm. csharp and js write the pair of `typeof`
//     tests nested, java/dart/swift write them with `&&`. Same predicate.
//  2. NOT A DIFFERENCE - the numeric coercion, already rtkNum: csNum / jvNum /
//     dtNum / swNum / jvToNumber, the same knob js_jadd took.
//  3. **DEFECT, in java, in BOTH ENGINES, with two DIFFERENT wrong answers.**
//     The oracle answers the SENTINEL 2 when either operand coerces to NaN, and
//     says at the site that it means "every relation is false". csCompare and
//     jvCmpPrim carry the sentinel; jpCompare, dtJsCompare and swLoose do not -
//     they fall through to `return 0`, i.e. EQUAL. Read by a caller that spells
//     `c <= 0` and `c >= 0`, 0 makes `<=` and `>=` TRUE for a NaN; the sentinel
//     read as an ordering makes `>` and `>=` TRUE. Java's two halves were on
//     opposite sides of that, so they disagreed with each other AND with java 24
//     on six of fourteen probe rows. JLS 15.20.1 is the same rule as ECMA-334
//     12.12.1: for <, <=, > and >= a NaN operand makes the result false.
//     Fixed by merging onto the oracle's sentinel and guarding it in js_jvcmp,
//     with abnf/jsrtjava.go:467 taking the same three lines. See the measured
//     table at js_jvcmp in java-rt.metajs.
//  4. KNOB - what the CALLER does with the sentinel, which is why this body
//     answers 2 rather than swallowing it. java and csharp turn it into false
//     for all four operators; dart's js_dart{lt,le,gt,ge} do the same; swift's
//     swRel has its own NaN marker (0 - 2) and translates. A shared body that
//     returned false itself could not serve swMinMax, which wants an ordering.
//  5. DOCUMENTED OMISSION - the oracle's `rt.hasBigInt` arm (bigPair, jsrt.go:
//     1545) compares two big.Ints before coercing. None of these four languages
//     turns hasBigInt on in this path; python does have a bignum and does not
//     reach jsCompare. Not carried, and not a difference between the four.
function rtCompare(l, r) {
    if (typeof l == "string" && typeof r == "string") {
        if (l < r) { return 0 - 1 }
        if (l > r) { return 1 }
        return 0
    }
    var a = rtkNum(l)
    var b = rtkNum(r)
    if (a !== a) { return 2 }
    if (b !== b) { return 2 }
    if (a < b) { return 0 - 1 }
    if (a > b) { return 1 }
    return 0
}

// ============================================ the clamped substring ========
//
// Go: rt.jpSubstring / rt.csSubstring, the same eight lines twice. java's
// String.substring and C#'s String.Substring both THROW on an out-of-range
// index; both grammars clamp instead, so the shared body is the clamp and the
// difference between the two languages is upstream of it. csSubstring and
// jpSubstring were byte-identical - ZERO knobs, and both files already import
// this one, so sharing it taxes no other language (see runtime-decimal.metajs's
// head for what that tax is and how it was measured).
function rtSubstring(s, b, e) {
    var begin = b
    var end = e
    if (begin < 0) { begin = 0 }
    if (end > s.length) { end = s.length }
    if (end < begin) { end = begin }
    return s.substring(begin, end)
}

// ============================================ the FIFTH pass (2026-08-04) ===
//
// Everything below was merged after the `scope_find` hash index landed
// (runtime-next-plan.md item 0) and the DECLARATION TAX that governed every
// earlier merge decision was re-measured at ZERO. Sizing is no longer an
// argument: a body a language imports and cannot call costs it nothing at
// either end of the module (docs/runtime-merge-plan.md, "THE THIRD ASKING").
// Each merge below carries its difference list, which is the only thing that
// still decides.

// A fresh copy of an array - Go's `append([]interface{}{}, xs...)`. There is no
// Array.prototype.slice in this dialect, so everyone wrote the loop.
//
// SEVEN bodies, in six languages, and the difference list is EMPTY: csCopyArr,
// dtCopyArr, k4bArgArr, k4Copy (kotlin had it twice, 993 lines apart),
// pyFCopyArr, rbCopyArr and swCopyArr were byte-for-byte identical down to the
// loop variable. The three names they went under - "copy an array", "the
// variadic tail of a closure", "the dict keys/values arms' defensive copy" -
// are three CALLERS of one function, which is exactly how seven copies of nine
// lines went unnoticed by every name-based view for months.
function rtCopyArr(a) {
    var out = []
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        out.push(a[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// Membership in an array, by the floor's strict equality.
//
// THREE bodies, ZERO knobs: dart's dtSeen (a cycle-breaking seen-list in the
// renderer), php's phNameIn (a key-list membership test in loose ==) and ruby's
// rbIsLambda (an identity scan of the lambda side table). rbIsLambda read its
// array from a FILE GLOBAL rather than a parameter, which is why the shape scan
// showed three bodies and the extern-name view showed nothing at all.
//
// rtDictFind above is the same scan returning the index, and it is called and
// not re-spelled here: `===` in layer 2 is the floor's strict_eq, so identity
// membership and dict-key lookup ARE one question. See its head comment for the
// measurement that settled that.
function rtHas(a, v) { return rtDictFind(a, v) >= 0 }

// The identity-keyed SIDE TABLE lookup: two parallel arrays scanned with `===`,
// answering vals[i] or undefined. MetaJS has no map keyed by a function value,
// so every language that has to remember something ABOUT a closure builds this.
//
// THREE bodies, ZERO knobs: kotlin's k4NameOf (ktFuncNames, jsrtkotlin.go:5764),
// python's pyBFuncName (the name a def bound a closure under) and js-rt's
// jvCtorDescOf (the class descriptor of a constructor function). TWO are merged.
//
// js-rt's is DECLINED, and not on the body: js-rt.metajs does not import this
// file, it defines a `js_supercall` of its own (a straight collision), and
// runtime.metajs requires seven rtk* hooks it would have to grow. Eight lines do
// not buy js-rt's first import of the shared runtime. Cross-referenced at
// js-rt.metajs's jvCtorDescOf so a change to one is seen at the other.
function rtPairGet(keys, vals, k) {
    var i = rtDictFind(keys, k)
    if (i < 0) { return undefined }
    return vals[i]
}

// An operand of INTEGER arithmetic: a Char box is one - it is an integral type
// in C#, Java and Kotlin - and a boxed double is not. Both a sized integer
// (tag 13) and a boxed double (tag 14) answer `typeof v == "number"` in the C
// floor, which is why the tag 14 test is explicit.
//
// THREE bodies, ZERO knobs, and TWO OF THEM WERE IN ONE FILE: kotlin-rt.metajs
// carried ktIsInt at line 229 and k4bIsIntegral at line 4384, and
// runtime-jvm.metajs already exported the same five lines as rtjIsIntegral for
// csharp and java. The only apparent difference was the char test - ktIsChar
// against rtIsCharBox - and `function ktIsChar(v) { return rtIsCharBox(v) }` is
// the whole of ktIsChar. Moved UP here from runtime-jvm.metajs rather than
// kotlin importing that file, because kotlin has neither of runtime-jvm's two
// knobs (rtjkCmp, rtjkFloOperand) and does not want its other five bodies.
function rtIsIntegral(v) {
    if (rtIsCharBox(v)) { return true }
    if (typeof v != "number") { return false }
    return !floIs(v)
}

// The number of CODE POINTS in a string - Go's len([]rune(s)) - so a surrogate
// pair counts once. TWO bodies, ZERO knobs: dart's dtStrLen and kotlin's
// k2RuneLen differed in the name of the second local (`d` against `t`) and in
// nothing else. The value model is UTF-16 code units throughout layer 2 (see
// docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md), so this walk is the only way to ask.
function rtRuneLen(s) {
    var n = 0
    var i = 0
    while (i < s.length) {
        var c = s.charCodeAt(i)
        if (c >= 55296 && c <= 56319 && i + 1 < s.length) {
            var d = s.charCodeAt(i + 1)
            if (d >= 56320 && d <= 57343) { i = i + 1 }
        }
        n = n + 1
        i = i + 1
    }
    return n
}

// A run of n "0"s. TWO bodies, ZERO knobs: php's phZeros and the rtZeros that
// used to live in runtime-decimal.metajs. It is HERE and not there because php
// has no decimal bignum and importing runtime-decimal.metajs for nine lines
// would be the tax argument in its worst form; runtime-decimal's own three
// importers (python, ruby, swift) all import this file first.
function rtZeros(n) {
    var out = ""
    var i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        out = out + "0"
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// rtIsObj plus the array rejection. TWO bodies, ZERO knobs: pyIsObj and rbIsObj
// were byte-identical, and both are the four tests rtIsObj's own head comment
// names - it merged the first three and deliberately left the fourth out,
// because ITS callers walk __class/__super and an array carries neither. Python
// and Ruby ask a stricter question in ~60 places each (`is this a user object
// rather than a list`), so the fourth test is real for them and the two files
// keep their own names as one-line delegations to this body.
function rtIsObjNotArr(v) {
    if (!rtIsObj(v)) { return false }
    return !Array.isArray(v)
}
